# 01 — His Legacy

## English

# His Legacy

Some lives are remembered for what they achieved.

Others are remembered for **how they made people feel**.

Balarama Krishna belonged to the latter.

He did not live a life surrounded by titles or great possessions. He lived a life built on **hard work, honesty, responsibility, family and relationships**.

He was an electrician by profession, but that was only one part of who he was. To his family, he was the person who carried their responsibilities on his shoulders. To the people of his village, he was someone they could trust. To many, he was not even addressed by his name—he was **Babai, Mamayya, Bava, Tatayya**—names that reflected the place he held in their hearts.

He had dreams of his own. He once wished to serve the nation by joining the Indian Army. Although a fracture prevented that dream from becoming reality, the qualities behind that dream—**discipline, courage, determination and a willingness to serve**—remained with him throughout his life.

His life was also made of simple things.

A cup of tea.

A newspaper at a familiar tea shop.

A day's work.

A conversation with someone he knew.

A responsibility fulfilled.

A family waiting for him to come home.

These ordinary moments became extraordinary because they were **his**.

And then, when life placed him in his final and most difficult moment, he gave one more thing.

**He gave what he could still give.**

Through organ and eye donation, his final act became a gift to others. His liver, two kidneys and two eyes were donated, giving **five individuals** the opportunity for a second life or restored vision.

His heart could not ultimately be donated, despite initially positive test results, but even in that final chapter, his intention remained one of giving.

His name was formally recognized by **Jeevandan AP**, and his eye donation was acknowledged by **L V Prasad Eye Institute**.

But perhaps the greatest measure of his legacy is not written on a certificate.

It lives in the people who remember him.

In the family that carries his values forward.

In the people who still remember calling him by affectionate names.

In the stories that will be told about him.

And in the lives that continue because, even in death, **he gave something of himself to others**.

His life reminds us that a meaningful life does not have to be extraordinary in the eyes of the world.

Sometimes, it is enough to **work honestly, love deeply, carry your responsibilities, treat people with kindness, and leave behind something that helps another person live.**

That was his legacy.

**Kanagala Balarama Krishna may no longer walk among us, but the values he lived by, the relationships he built, the memories he created, and the lives touched by his final act continue to carry his presence forward.**

His journey ended.

**His legacy did not.**

