# A Final Message From Your Family

**Dear Nanna,**

We still find it difficult to believe that you are no longer going to walk through that door.

There are moments when we still expect you to come home after work.

There are moments when we hear something and instinctively think, *“We should tell Nanna about this.”*

And then, for a moment, reality reminds us that you are no longer here in the way you once were.

We miss you.

We miss your presence.

We miss your voice.

We miss the way you responded when we called you **“Nanna.”**

We miss the ordinary things that we once never thought we would have to miss.

You spent your life working for us.

You carried the responsibilities of our family without making them feel like burdens.

You worked as an electrician, travelled for work, built relationships wherever you went, and returned home to the family that was always waiting for you.

You taught us many things without sitting down to teach us.

You taught us through the way you lived.

You showed us the value of **honesty**.

You showed us the meaning of **hard work**.

You showed us how important it is to stand by our family.

And perhaps most importantly, you showed us how to remain connected to people.

You were called **Babai, Mamayya, Bava and Tatayya** by people who were not merely acquaintances, but people who felt that you belonged to them.

That was something special about you.

You made people feel that they were family.

We will always remember the simple things that made you who you were.

Your love for tea.

Your habit of sitting at tea shops and reading the newspaper.

Your everyday routines.

Your work.

Your conversations.

Your presence at home.

We never imagined that these ordinary memories would one day become some of our most precious possessions.

And then came **3 August 2025**.

A day that began like every other day.

You left home after your morning routine, telling Amma that you would return and have your breakfast.

We never imagined that those would become the last ordinary words of that kind we would remember.

The days that followed changed our lives forever.

We prayed.

We hoped.

We waited.

We refused to believe that we could lose you.

Even when the doctors gave us almost no hope, our hearts continued to hold on to that tiny possibility that you would somehow come back to us.

And when that hope finally disappeared, we were left with a pain that words cannot explain.

But even in that darkest moment, something beautiful came from the person you had always been.

You gave.

You gave your organs.

You gave your eyes.

And through that final act, **five individuals were given the opportunity for a second life or restored vision.**

We lost you.

But somewhere in this world, lives continue because of you.

That thought does not erase our pain.

It never will.

But it gives us something to hold on to.

It gives us a reason to feel proud of you even through our tears.

Nanna, we wish you had stayed.

We wish we could have had more mornings with you.

More conversations.

More cups of tea.

More ordinary days.

More moments that we would never have realized were precious until they were gone.

But since we cannot change what happened, we will carry you with us in the only way we can.

**We will remember you.**

We will speak about you.

We will tell your stories.

We will preserve your memories.

We will carry forward the values you lived by.

And whenever someone asks us who you were, we will never simply say:

*"He was our father."*

We will say:

**He was a hardworking man.
He was an honest man.
He was a loving husband.
He was a devoted father.
He was a brother.
He was a friend.
He was Babai, Mamayya, Bava and Tatayya to many.
And in the end, he became a giver of life.**

You may no longer be here to hear us say it.

But we hope that wherever your memory lives, you know this:

**We are proud of you, Nanna.**

We love you.

We miss you.

And we will carry you with us—

**for the rest of our lives.**

**Until our last breath,
you will remain a part of us.**

